00:07:17.633 --> 00:07:18.550
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:20.550
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You listed
a lot of foundations there

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:21.690
and a lot of different teams.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:22.270
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.478
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Let's
start with the highest level.

00:07:24.478 --> 00:07:26.410
What is the purpose of
the Linux Foundation?

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:26.680
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Cool.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:27.380
Very simple.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:30.113
So if you like go pull up
the bylaws and charter,

00:07:30.113 --> 00:07:31.780
the Linux Foundation
is basically tasked

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.260
to support and sustain
the development of Linux

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.600
and open source software.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.710
And that open source
software is very broad.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:42.430
So initially, Linux Foundation
started out very focused

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.200
on Linux, like
Linus and Greg KH,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.850
the stable and
unstable maintainers

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:48.145
are employed as fellows
and go hack on the kernel

00:07:48.145 --> 00:07:49.520
and make sure to
take care of it.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.570
But eventually what happened,
as Linux grew and just

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:54.550
in general open
source grew, people

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.717
were trying to find ways
to kind of collaborate.

00:07:56.717 --> 00:07:58.300
And if you kind of
look at it, there's

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.008
a lot of different
foundations out there.

00:08:00.008 --> 00:08:01.840
There's Apache, but
their kind of motto

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.450
is very much focused
on the individual

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.600
and they kind of pretend
companies don't exist.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.430
It's very individual community,
and they're volunteer runs.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.650
So there's no
necessary services.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.293
The Linux Foundation is more
of a professional services

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:15.835
kind of foundation,
where we actually

00:08:15.835 --> 00:08:19.030
have people on staff that
help with marketing, DevRel,

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.870
technical writing, all the
stuff that generally project

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:23.620
maintainers don't like
to do but are super

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.420
useful for building community.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:27.970
Running events, we're
putting on KubeCon Barcelona

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:30.890
in a month and a half, soon,
and it's almost a 10,000 person

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:31.390
event.

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:33.730
That's a lot to put
on, but actually super

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:35.049
useful to build communities.

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:37.627
So we are kind of a full
service professional staff

00:08:37.627 --> 00:08:38.169
organization.

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:39.853
We've grown to
support open source

00:08:39.853 --> 00:08:41.020
in all different industries.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.120
People would come to
us and say, you know,

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:44.620
Toyota, hey, we
want to do something

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.250
in the automotive industry
to bring open source to cars,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.442
to make things more
efficient and innovative.

00:08:49.442 --> 00:08:50.900
We've worked at
the telco industry,

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.525
so we have Linux
Foundation networking.

00:08:52.525 --> 00:08:54.490
We cleaned up the mess
with the [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.480
and merged those things
into one organization.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.090
So we've kind of
just grown through--

00:08:58.090 --> 00:08:59.930
Linux was one of early
open source projects,

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.390
and people naturally came to
our organization for help.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:02.820
MARK MANDEL: So you said--

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:03.910
I mean, you said support.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.820
And then you've also
listed a wide variety

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.760
of very interesting things that
you do for open source projects

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:10.660
as a whole.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:11.803
Is there any limit to that?

00:09:11.803 --> 00:09:13.970
Like, how does that get
coord-- like, what happens--

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:14.380
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
Yeah, so I mean,

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:16.250
think about each kind
of sub-foundation,

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:17.890
it kind of runs
independently, right?

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.348
They have their own-- you could
think of it like a business

00:09:20.348 --> 00:09:22.330
unit, their own PNL and
potentially own staff.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:24.100
But the parent entity
is the Linux Foundation.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.070
So they kind of basically
work with the community

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.278
to figure out what they need.

00:09:27.278 --> 00:09:29.260
I kind of have a saying
that each open source

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.360
project is unhappy in
their own unique way.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.880
And you kind of have to work
with them to figure out,

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.770
maybe you need
infrastructure help.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:37.960
A lot of the telco
focused foundations,

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:41.450
they have physical devices, like
routers and other crazy stuff.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.900
And so you kind of need a lab
to kind of help them with.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:45.632
Others may just
need help with-- we

00:09:45.632 --> 00:09:46.840
don't like technical writing.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:47.510
We kind of suck at it.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:48.680
Can you help us with that?

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.500
So we do kind of a
variety of things

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.467
based on each type of project.

00:09:52.467 --> 00:09:54.550
But the LF's a fairly
decently large organization.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:57.460
Like, we also host things like--
people forget letsencrypt.org.

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:59.140
You know, everyone
loves free SSL certs.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:00.416
We basically helped--

00:10:00.416 --> 00:10:00.610
MARK MANDEL: Thank you.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:01.985
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
--bootstrap that

00:10:01.985 --> 00:10:03.985
and sustain it, essentially,
as an organization.

00:10:03.985 --> 00:10:05.443
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So I mean, that's

00:10:05.443 --> 00:10:07.130
still a long list of
things that you do.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:08.380
But it's really interesting
when you mentioned

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:10.463
that groups or open source
groups will come to you

00:10:10.463 --> 00:10:12.130
and say, we need
some help doing this.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:12.490
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yep.

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:13.780
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
How does that group

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:16.240
OK with working with like, hey,
let's just sign everything over

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:17.032
to this foundation?

00:10:17.032 --> 00:10:17.948
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah.

00:10:17.948 --> 00:10:19.930
So I mean think about
it, businesses inherently

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.385
don't trust each other,
right, in many ways, right?

00:10:22.385 --> 00:10:24.760
They feel like, oh, you're
going to sue me for something,

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:27.343
or unless there's some type of
agreement or partnership, well,

00:10:27.343 --> 00:10:29.480
by putting code and
some level of IP,

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.273
whether it's a trademark
in a neutral place

00:10:31.273 --> 00:10:32.440
like we did with Kubernetes.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.600
We have this wonderful
conformance program

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.430
that everyone works
with and ensures

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:38.982
that they respect the
project and are conformant.

00:10:38.982 --> 00:10:41.440
We set these types of things
up for different organizations

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.810
for different industries,
verticals, and projects.

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:47.920
So having a neutral entity and
the IP owned in a neutral way

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.710
builds trust across
these companies.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.540
And they decide
together what they

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:53.970
want to work on together, like
what part of the industry,

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:55.928
maybe, is commoditized
at some layer,

00:10:55.928 --> 00:10:58.470
or some interesting thing they
kind of want to chase and work

00:10:58.470 --> 00:10:59.050
on together.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.720
So essentially,
neutrality builds trust,

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.078
and to me is a cornerstone
of working together.

00:11:03.078 --> 00:11:05.370
MARK MANDEL: So you handle
intercompany politics really

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:05.870
well.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:06.972
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:06.972 --> 00:11:08.430
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
So I will tell you

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.020
the sad thing about this job.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.030
It unfortunately makes
you respect politicians

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.080
a little bit more.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:13.942
And that's painful.

00:11:13.942 --> 00:11:16.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, I
mean, it highlights, right?

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:18.608
Like, I'm sure there are and
there have been no shortage of

00:11:18.608 --> 00:11:20.190
and probably
currently are plenty

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.000
of conflicts between
different people

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.250
wanting to go different ways--

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:24.230
MARK MANDEL:
Different incentives.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:24.900
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
--and different people

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:25.830
in the community
who are just like,

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.997
I don't know if I want to
be governed by some higher

00:11:27.997 --> 00:11:29.360
authority that I didn't choose.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:30.060
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
Yeah, or they compete

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:31.470
at the market at some
level and someone's

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.510
like, I want to go push this
feature into something that I

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:34.330
may sell.

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.990
But at the end of the day,
like, we very much focus

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.180
on open source projects
and making sure

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.362
they are very healthy,
sustained, and do everything

00:11:41.362 --> 00:11:42.570
to make sure they're healthy.

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:45.820
Our members build products
that compete in the market

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:47.820
and go fight it out and
figure out what they do.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:49.530
And so we just--
our whole goal--

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.960
we kind of have the saying of
projects, products, profit.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:53.610
So we sustain projects.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.220
Our members build products,
sell them, make profits.

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:58.500
And that gets funneled
back into the foundation.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:00.450
MARK MANDEL: How do
people get involved?

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.460
If I've got a little
project where I'm like,

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.502
hey, I wrote a little
Go library and I

00:12:04.502 --> 00:12:06.210
want to put that in
the Linux Foundation,

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:07.950
I'm assuming that's
not something I can do.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.300
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: You
know, there is a process,

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:10.920
if you actually go to the
Linux Foundation org website,

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:12.270
there's propose a
project and there's

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:13.437
kind of like a little forum.

00:12:13.437 --> 00:12:15.210
But recently we
announced an initiative

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:17.190
called communitybridge.org.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:20.100
So maybe you don't need a
full blown crazy foundation,

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.950
but you may want to have
support to maybe raise

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:25.440
a little funding for
a meetup or an event,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.000
or you may want
to do internships.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.400
So we kind of even have a,
quote unquote, "summer code

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:30.900
as a service" for
lack of a better--

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:31.350
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:33.170
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: --analogy
that could go all year round.

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:34.920
So we have that
available for people

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.390
that are just
individual projects

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:38.898
and may not have, like,
crazy backing or something.

00:12:38.898 --> 00:12:40.440
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
so I know recently

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.660
I believe you've
formed a coalition

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.150
or whatever you want to call
it for the media association

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:45.520
or something--

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:46.980
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: The
Academy Software Foundation.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:48.030
MARK MANDEL: That's the
one I'm looking for.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:48.870
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: For
the film industry.

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:49.380
MARK MANDEL: Thank you.

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:50.520
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: They don't
want to collaborate either,

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:51.880
but they've decided,
you know what?

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:53.490
Maybe we should
start not reinventing

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:55.830
all these, like, color
tools and other things.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.540
And let's collaborate
on the basis,

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:58.500
because what we're
actually good at

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.360
is stories and making
movies, not actually

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:01.560
the software to make those.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.060
MARK MANDEL: How
long did that take?

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:04.185
What was that process like?

00:13:04.185 --> 00:13:05.580
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Two-plus years.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.050
You know, generally
when these things start,

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:08.400
they don't happen overnight.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:10.980
Usually one person that
wants to champion open source

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:13.230
in an organization has
realized, like, this is stupid.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.750
We're reinventing the
wheel all over the place.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.090
Comes to us or somehow we
run into them at an event

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:19.620
and we just start
the discussions.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.650
You know, the Linux Foundation
has over 1,000 members

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:23.290
worldwide.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:24.250
And so we just, like, reach out.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:25.260
Oh, you know this person here?

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.930
And then we kind of maybe host
a little event or a meet-up.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:30.970
And naturally people come
together and write a mission,

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.300
vision, and maybe a
foundation is formed.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:34.530
Or maybe it isn't.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:35.905
So sometimes it
doesn't work out.

00:13:35.905 --> 00:13:38.530
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, I mean,
that's not unique to the media.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.610
There's a lot of industries
that still prefer very private,

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.578
kind of closed door,
not open conversations.

00:13:43.578 --> 00:13:45.870
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: We're actually
seeing other industries

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:46.470
like pharma.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.460
They don't really like to talk
to each other much, either.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.340
And they're starting to dabble,
potentially, in open source.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.380
And so we're just
like into this area

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:54.870
where we're one of the early
pioneers of open source

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.670
as an organization,
and we essentially

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.760
act as foundation as a service
and bring communities together

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.640
that want to collaborate on
some specific piece of software.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:03.750
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
What's the sell there?

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:05.250
I mean, you're not
selling anything,

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.320
because ultimately,
you're not paying, right?

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:09.630
But like, how do you go to
and approach these people?

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:10.800
Or do they just come to you?

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:13.050
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Just naturally
come to us, generally.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:14.860
So there's no, like,
proactive reach out.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:16.650
It's usually the
problems are actually

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:19.900
coming from the businesses
or people that have them.

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.750
So we're not actively
going out and saying,

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:23.070
you should do open source.

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:24.510
Open source is great.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:25.290
MARK MANDEL: What
sort of problems

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.240
do you think they're trying
to solve with open source?

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:28.680
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Cross
company collaboration.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:30.510
So generally there's
some piece of the stack

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:32.070
that they may want
to have commoditized,

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:34.140
just like the Academy Software
Foundation, all these color

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.510
tools that they've come up
with they're reinventing

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:39.120
on their own, and their
company policies do not

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.265
allow them to easily collaborate
with different organizations.

00:14:42.265 --> 00:14:43.890
So if you put the
code in a foundation,

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.920
there's usually an IP policy
that everyone agrees to,

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.290
a fair governance policy
that everyone agrees to

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.475
of how maintainers are
elected and removed.

00:14:51.475 --> 00:14:53.100
And then all of a
sudden, the companies

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:55.490
could collaborate
together in a fair way.

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:57.240
That's, like, a sports
match or something,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.400
where everyone knows
how the rules of hockey

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:01.590
work and there's a
referee or something.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:02.393
So let's go at it.

00:15:02.393 --> 00:15:03.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Do you also work

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.090
in the other way, where
you may go into companies

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:07.650
and educate them
about open source

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:09.630
and, like, how they can work
in that arena in a better way?

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:10.547
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: 100%.

00:15:10.547 --> 00:15:12.540
Half my job is
probably educating

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.125
companies that are
new to open source

00:15:15.125 --> 00:15:16.500
or don't understand
how it works.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:18.840
It's actually-- my personal
vision of open source

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:20.340
is sustained by
convincing companies

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.548
that, A, open source is a
good thing that they should

00:15:22.548 --> 00:15:24.540
contribute to, either
by hiring maintainers

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:26.433
or contributing to
projects and just being

00:15:26.433 --> 00:15:27.600
involved in the communities.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.640
And we tend to live a
little bit in the bubble

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.920
sometimes in kind of like
the Silicon Valley area.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:35.348
But if you go out to automotive
companies or pharma companies,

00:15:35.348 --> 00:15:37.140
they haven't heard of
Kubernetes, you know,

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.540
bless their heart.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:41.310
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I can't imagine--

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:43.510
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah,
open source is new to them,

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:44.843
and so we do a lot of education.

00:15:44.843 --> 00:15:47.469
We have free resources,
guides that we produce.

00:15:47.469 --> 00:15:49.886
I co-founded one initiative
called the todogroup.org where

00:15:49.886 --> 00:15:52.680
I used to run the open source
office at Twitter where we just

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:55.470
produced a bunch of material
of, like, how to contribute

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.113
open source, how
to start a project,

00:15:57.113 --> 00:15:58.530
how to work with
others, you know,

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:00.460
what happens when things
go wrong in open source.

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.710
And so we just open sourced
all these things available

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:04.212
for companies to
learn from and use.

00:16:04.212 --> 00:16:05.670
MARK MANDEL: I know
I've definitely

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:07.920
run into companies where I
know members who would just

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:10.325
be like, I can't even write
feedback on a GitHub project

00:16:10.325 --> 00:16:12.450
because, like, legal or PR
is not going to like it.

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:13.210
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: You
have to go through the 11

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:14.660
circles of legal and PR--

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:15.660
MARK MANDEL: Every time.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:17.160
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah,
every time, for a patch

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:17.910
or for everything.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:18.300
Yep.

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.130
MARK MANDEL: But I think you do
some legal services, as well?

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:20.350
Is that right?

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:20.700
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah, we do.

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:23.285
We have legal staff and
external counsel on trademarks

00:16:23.285 --> 00:16:24.660
and just legal
advice in general.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.160
Like, the Linux Foundation, by--

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:27.390
you join the organization,
there's actually

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:29.015
a legal network and
they have, I think,

00:16:29.015 --> 00:16:31.710
conferences twice a year where
all these lawyers go into room,

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:32.910
which I want to avoid.

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:35.390
But they talk to each other
and they learn from each other.

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:36.600
That's just kind of
one benefit of being

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:37.642
part of the organization.

00:16:37.642 --> 00:16:39.540
And usually a huge
function of what we do

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:42.015
is education of how
open source works.

00:16:42.015 --> 00:16:44.140
MARK MANDEL: Actually,
that's an interesting point.

00:16:44.140 --> 00:16:46.300
People can still be
part of the foundation,

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:48.580
but they don't necessarily
have to open source code.

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:49.620
They may just be
there so that they

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:51.220
can interact with open
source code in a healthy way.

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:52.637
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:16:52.637 --> 00:16:56.030
I mean, all our projects don't
require any form of membership,

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:56.530
right?

00:16:56.530 --> 00:16:58.950
Like, all technical stuff, you
just show up and contribute,

00:16:58.950 --> 00:16:59.450
you know.

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:00.750
It's a duocracy, essentially.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:02.220
Like, if you show
up, maybe you'll

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:03.595
become a maintainer
or something.

00:17:03.595 --> 00:17:05.700
The funding there is just
to sustain us, like--

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:10.390
it's not cheap to put on KubeCon
or technical writers and so on.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:12.849
So that's kind of where the
resources go to sustain that.

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:14.220
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So that's
a pretty helpful overview

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:15.930
of why the Linux
Foundation is important

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:17.500
and some of the reasons
that a lot of people

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.140
might look at some of
the projects there.

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:20.579
But you mentioned that you've
been spending a lot of time

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:21.440
on CNCF.

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:24.329
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah, I
was employee one start--

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:26.185
with CNCF and the open
container initiative

00:17:26.185 --> 00:17:27.810
at the same time,
which if you remember

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.392
the wonderful container
wars of days old.

00:17:30.392 --> 00:17:32.100
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Are
we settled on that?

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:32.648
Is that good?

00:17:32.648 --> 00:17:34.440
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: OCI--
we have containers--

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:35.357
we have an image spec.

00:17:35.357 --> 00:17:36.660
We have a runtime spec.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.010
The team is actively working
on a distribution spec.

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:41.100
So oh my God, wouldn't it
be amazing if registries

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:42.823
had the same kind
of API out there

00:17:42.823 --> 00:17:45.240
and there wasn't any crazy
issues where one didn't support

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:46.560
multi arc support or something?

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.810
So they are, like, working
together on those problems.

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:50.280
And they've made
a lot of progress.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:52.568
Almost every container
runtime supports the OCI image

00:17:52.568 --> 00:17:53.860
and runtime spec at some level.

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:54.300
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
I was going to say,

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:56.610
you were, like,
number 1 at CNCF.

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:57.790
Where did that come from?

00:17:57.790 --> 00:17:58.305
What was the need?

00:17:58.305 --> 00:17:58.960
Why did that come about?

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:01.252
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: No, it's
kind of an interesting story.

00:18:01.252 --> 00:18:03.540
I spent five years
before joining

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.790
the Linux Foundation in
Twitter working on open source

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:06.500
infrastructure.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:10.350
So we had our containers, Mesos,
before they were really cool.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:12.790
I quit that job after
five years of startup.

00:18:12.790 --> 00:18:13.290
Good times.

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:13.913
Learned a lot.

00:18:13.913 --> 00:18:14.580
And then I was--

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:15.230
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Living
in the valley, right?

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:15.960
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah, exactly.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:16.450
Exactly.

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:17.650
I was like, I need a
little time off after that.

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:19.358
And then had this
wonderful conversation

00:18:19.358 --> 00:18:21.400
with the head of the Linux
Foundation Jim Zelmin,

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:22.942
who called me and
was like, you know,

00:18:22.942 --> 00:18:24.510
Google has open
source Kubernetes.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:25.530
You know, there's
a bunch of folks

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:27.210
that want to build a
standard around containers.

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:28.050
You want to come help this?

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:30.390
You've had a lot of experience
in open source and so on.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:30.900
It's great.

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:31.983
You know, we're nonprofit.

00:18:31.983 --> 00:18:33.390
It's going to be super easy.

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:36.027
Just come do this,
and you know--

00:18:36.027 --> 00:18:37.860
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's
basically a vacation.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:38.770
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah,
it's going to be easy.

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:40.650
Oh, not for profit, like,
you get like 30 vacation

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:41.180
days or something.

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:42.000
It's going to be great.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:43.680
You know, it turned
out to be super--

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:45.680
I don't want to say
painful, but very rewarding,

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:46.890
like, bringing together--

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:47.160
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Challenging.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:48.480
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Challenging
is probably the better word.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.790
But bringing together
competitors to, like,

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:52.260
essentially
collaborate and decide

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.870
which parts of the stack,
essentially, we actually

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:57.540
want to commoditize or agree
that we should standardize

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:58.857
is a fun experiment.

00:18:58.857 --> 00:19:00.190
And it shows kind of the values.

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:03.453
I don't think if we did not do
OCI and help grow Kubernetes

00:19:03.453 --> 00:19:05.370
where it is today, it
wouldn't have worked out

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:08.050
without getting that agreement
on container formats and so on.

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.050
Otherwise, like, if you
go to a different cloud,

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:11.550
you have to go, like,
rebuild your containers

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:12.467
or use another format.

00:19:12.467 --> 00:19:14.010
It would be a nightmare.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:14.340
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Yeah, I mean, I

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:16.050
think that's a
very valuable point

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:17.880
to why these things exist.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:20.027
Again, going back to why
these foundations are

00:19:20.027 --> 00:19:20.985
kind of helping people.

00:19:20.985 --> 00:19:21.360
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:22.860
And I think the
other useful thing

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:25.842
is, I do want to say, like,
when Craig McLuckie and Sarah

00:19:25.842 --> 00:19:27.300
Novotny and some
of the other folks

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:29.592
that were very early involved
with the foundation is we

00:19:29.592 --> 00:19:31.980
made a deliberate decision
not to just call it

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:33.157
the Kubernetes Foundation.

00:19:33.157 --> 00:19:35.490
It was going to be the Cloud
Native Computing Foundation

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:39.140
and encompass more than just one
specific project or technology.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:42.258
It was to come up with a
kind of, here's our mission.

00:19:42.258 --> 00:19:44.550
We want to spread Cloud
Native, bring projects together

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:45.967
that are very
useful in the space.

00:19:45.967 --> 00:19:48.720
We don't necessarily want to
anoint one technically project

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:49.220
as a winner.

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:51.095
We kind of want to build
some specifications.

00:19:51.095 --> 00:19:52.345
And it worked out pretty well.

00:19:52.345 --> 00:19:54.553
I think we're a lot more
successful with that mission

00:19:54.553 --> 00:19:56.070
than just specifically
saying, we're

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:57.560
just the Kubernetes Foundation.

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:01.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So you were
employee number one CNCF.

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:03.540
What does Cloud
Native mean to you?

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:04.800
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Oh my gosh.

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:06.780
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, we ask the
hard questions on this podcast.

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:07.470
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: No, it's great.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:08.520
So you know, what's
kind of funny

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.770
is when we started the
organization it was like,

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:12.900
Cloud Native is
all about services

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.850
that live in containers
that are orchestrated

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:15.990
by some central process.

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:17.340
And that was kind of like
the originally thing that

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:18.730
was baked into the charter.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:21.120
But recently, we had this
funny debate within the CNCF

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:23.523
technical community of,
maybe we should write down

00:20:23.523 --> 00:20:24.690
the definition or update it.

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:26.160
And so it's actually
been updated.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:27.240
It's on GitHub.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.320
It's actually much
longer than I like.

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:31.800
But essentially, it encompasses
that original definition,

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:34.140
but it includes more
things like service meshes,

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:35.710
concept resiliency, and so on.

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:37.170
So if I had my computer in
front of me, I could read it.

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:37.490
But--

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:37.890
MARK MANDEL: That's OK.

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:39.223
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: It is too long.

00:20:39.223 --> 00:20:41.820
But it is something
that the community of--

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:44.040
we have hundreds of
members, thousands

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:46.710
of developers that actually
came up, agreed upon, and voted

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:47.250
on it.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:49.770
It's translated into,
like, Chinese, Japanese,

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:50.770
Korean on GitHub.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:53.270
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Is that going
to continue to grow, though?

00:20:53.270 --> 00:20:55.520
I mean, especially as the
ecosystem kind of rapidly

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:57.860
is right now exploding
in every direction,

00:20:57.860 --> 00:21:00.980
how is the Foundation going
to be able to kind of oversee

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:01.970
a good chunk of that?

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:03.890
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: We're no
longer one person anymore.

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:04.840
There's a staff about--

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:05.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
That usually helps.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:06.120
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:07.745
There's a staff of
about 30 or so folks

00:21:07.745 --> 00:21:09.593
that work directly
on CNCF full time.

00:21:09.593 --> 00:21:10.760
We have a lovely booth here.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:11.880
I don't know if you swung by.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:12.920
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's great.

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:13.430
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah, exactly.

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:13.850
It's very pretty.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:14.892
We have lots of stickers.

00:21:14.892 --> 00:21:17.790
So if you're into, like, you
know Fluentd, containerd.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:19.310
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I have a
few stickers on my laptop.

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:19.520
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:22.420
I saw some podcast stickers
that somehow ended up on the--

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:22.670
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Strange.

00:21:22.670 --> 00:21:22.940
How did that--

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:23.440
MARK MANDEL: I don't know.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:24.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's weird.

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:25.520
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Exactly.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:27.110
We have a decent
amount of staff now.

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:29.068
So we have about a little
over 30 projects now.

00:21:29.068 --> 00:21:30.980
And as we grow as
an organization,

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:32.720
we try to staff
up appropriately.

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:35.730
And part of our work is not
only staffing the Foundation,

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:37.730
but also working with
companies to convince them

00:21:37.730 --> 00:21:39.920
that if you're betting your
business on Kubernetes,

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:41.753
you know, maybe you
should try to contribute

00:21:41.753 --> 00:21:45.380
some resources to translate the
Kubernetes docs into Japanese

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:46.800
or contribute in your own way.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:49.310
And a lot of companies
actually need that education

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:51.740
because they're very new
to open source, you know.

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:54.200
It's very surprising if
you're just living in the Bay

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:57.050
and sometimes you go talk to
a bunch of crazy customers

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:58.635
or people that are
new to open source.

00:21:58.635 --> 00:21:59.510
There's lots of them.

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:01.542
We're still kind of in
the early days of people

00:22:01.542 --> 00:22:03.250
understanding open
source, in my opinion.

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:03.650
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I mean.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:05.040
It's not-- I mean, it's
not necessarily crazy.

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:07.290
It's just different people at
different levels of the stack.

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:08.480
And that's just
where they're at.

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:09.800
And that makes sense, right?

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:11.840
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So I mean,
that sounds good from a like,

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:13.060
you can join this foundation.

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:14.210
You can get this support.

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:15.752
If you don't like
it, you don't join.

00:22:15.752 --> 00:22:16.280
Very simple.

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:17.680
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Or you could
still always contribute code.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:19.883
Like, if you want to
fiscally support it,

00:22:19.883 --> 00:22:21.050
you're more than welcome to.

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:22.868
But it's not a
requirement by any means.

00:22:22.868 --> 00:22:25.160
So if you're building, like,
Kubernetes conformant type

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:26.330
things, then yeah,
maybe you should

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:27.290
support this
organization cause you're

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:28.130
betting your business on it.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:30.047
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Have
you run into any people

00:22:30.047 --> 00:22:31.950
who are actually
resisting the Foundation?

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:32.810
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: It's more
of an education, like,

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:34.550
so what do you necessarily do?

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:37.043
Or it's like, oh, I have
this Kubernetes distribution.

00:22:37.043 --> 00:22:38.460
Oh, there's a
conformance program.

00:22:38.460 --> 00:22:39.530
Oh, it's run by CNCF.

00:22:39.530 --> 00:22:41.260
I had no idea that this existed.

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:43.760
So it's more of just educating
people on kind of what we do.

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:45.998
We produce these kind of
amazing annual reports.

00:22:45.998 --> 00:22:47.540
I don't know if
you've seen them yet,

00:22:47.540 --> 00:22:49.085
but if you go to the CNCF
website, go down there

00:22:49.085 --> 00:22:51.210
and find the latest annual
report, it kind of shows

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:53.810
the amount of events we put
on, the amount of diversity

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.400
scholarships that we've done
in the organization, the amount

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:57.480
of contributions we've
done as an organization.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:59.313
So we kind of just
publish all that

00:22:59.313 --> 00:23:00.980
and try to educate
people out there kind

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:02.690
of what the organization
does and how they

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:03.680
could kind of get involved.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:04.060
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.018
Sounds like there's a
lot of stuff that happens

00:23:06.018 --> 00:23:07.430
at the organizational level.

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:09.765
If I'm just a lone developer
typing away in my cube,

00:23:09.765 --> 00:23:11.140
what have you done
for me lately?

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:14.225
Like, what does the CNCF and
the Linux Foundation kind of

00:23:14.225 --> 00:23:15.350
do at the individual layer?

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:17.600
Or is it just more like,
I'm helping you build stuff

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:18.110
and that's cool?

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:18.800
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
It's a combination.

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:20.190
Everything on an
individual level,

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:21.690
it's like, we've
given out, I think,

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:26.387
last year 300-plus
diversity scholarships

00:23:26.387 --> 00:23:28.220
if you're an individual
coming from wherever

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:30.303
and, like, hey, I want to
attend my first KubeCon.

00:23:30.303 --> 00:23:31.550
So we've done a lot of that.

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:32.260
Like, it's hard.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:34.010
Like, from a Linux
Foundation perspective,

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:36.760
it's like, use letsencrypt.org,
get free SSL certs.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:37.040
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:37.610
That's very true.

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:38.210
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Exactly.

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:40.377
With the community bridge
platform that we launched,

00:23:40.377 --> 00:23:43.010
now we offer summer code as a
service any time for projects.

00:23:43.010 --> 00:23:44.510
And so you can go
have an internship

00:23:44.510 --> 00:23:47.490
anytime of the year, instead
of just a specific time.

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:49.040
So I think as a
politician we have

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:50.373
multiple constituents right?

00:23:50.373 --> 00:23:51.290
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:51.970
I like that.

00:23:51.970 --> 00:23:52.790
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
We have members.

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:54.230
We have individuals, developers.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:55.730
And so we try to
provide services

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:57.677
for all different
types of constituents

00:23:57.677 --> 00:23:59.510
because they have
different cares and needs.

00:23:59.510 --> 00:24:01.662
Back to my saying, like,
each open source project

00:24:01.662 --> 00:24:03.120
is unhappy in their
own unique way.

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:04.280
And we do our best
to kind of work

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:06.180
with different types
of people, companies,

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:07.710
and so on, to
address your needs.

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:08.600
MARK MANDEL: What
interesting trends

00:24:08.600 --> 00:24:10.490
are you seeing at the
moment across open source?

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:12.000
Are you seeing anything
that's changing

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:13.040
the landscape over time?

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:14.780
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: I don't want
to talk about the whole open

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:17.390
source licensing gate, you know,
stuff going on with companies

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:20.750
coming up with kind
of bespoke anti-type--

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:21.838
I'll say cloud licenses.

00:24:21.838 --> 00:24:23.130
I think it's just a long topic.

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:27.800
But the thing that I see the
most is new industries that you

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:29.510
would not think
would have anything

00:24:29.510 --> 00:24:32.150
to do with open source, you
know-- the automotive industry,

00:24:32.150 --> 00:24:34.127
the pharma industry--
cities even

00:24:34.127 --> 00:24:35.960
are starting to want
to collaborate and even

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:37.410
share data amongst each other.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:38.640
So a lot of trend of
like, new industries

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:40.580
that you typically would
not expect to do anything

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:42.247
with open source are
starting to realize

00:24:42.247 --> 00:24:44.360
that we would like to
collaborate, potentially,

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:46.970
with each other, and we kind of
want a fair and neutral place

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:47.470
to do that.

00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:49.887
So we're getting approached a
lot by these new industries,

00:24:49.887 --> 00:24:52.460
even the gaming industry, giving
back to our original meeting

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:53.008
at GDC.

00:24:53.008 --> 00:24:55.550
MARK MANDEL: What has been the
particularly challenging thing

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:56.300
that you've been working on?

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:57.620
Because I know-- I'm
guessing there's a lot,

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:59.203
I mean, working with
varying interests

00:24:59.203 --> 00:25:00.410
and various incentives.

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:02.827
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: I mean, I
think it's sometimes difficult.

00:25:02.827 --> 00:25:06.020
You know, as an organization, we
are still somewhat of a small,

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:06.810
non-for-profit.

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:09.685
So we are kind of limited
in resources in some ways

00:25:09.685 --> 00:25:11.060
when we kind of
start new things.

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:15.030
So recently, I was working
with Facebook on a project,

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:16.370
a technology called GraphQL.

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:17.930
Solves a very
interesting problem.

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:20.300
And there was a large
community of folks

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:24.050
out there that wanted to see
GraphQL in a neutral place.

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:26.090
And so it was a
year-plus project

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:28.550
to kind of make that
happen, you know, convince

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:30.230
the right parties,
work with lawyers,

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:33.700
and eventually that was kind
of done as like a side project.

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:35.950
And now that the Foundation
is finally kind of formed,

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:37.158
it could be properly staffed.

00:25:37.158 --> 00:25:39.290
So we kind of have this
weird bootstrapping problem

00:25:39.290 --> 00:25:41.498
where you know, someone will
come to us with an idea,

00:25:41.498 --> 00:25:43.528
and you know, let's just
work on this on my side

00:25:43.528 --> 00:25:45.320
and figure out what,
you know, eventually--

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:47.820
I'll make it work and then we'll
get it staffed appropriately.

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:50.240
So it's kind of a-- we have a
bit of a resource constraint

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:51.530
type problem when it
comes to new projects,

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:53.720
because we are kind of
fairly small compared

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:55.880
to these wonderful billion
dollar companies that

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:56.630
are all around me.

00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:57.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, exactly.

00:25:57.140 --> 00:25:58.160
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
What's on your wishlist?

00:25:58.160 --> 00:26:00.192
I mean, what are you
really hoping to see soon?

00:26:00.192 --> 00:26:02.150
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Like I
said, my focus is CNCF.

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:04.790
Like, keeping our
projects, you know, happy,

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:06.870
avoiding conflict
as much as possible,

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:08.840
making sure that
we continue to grow

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:10.370
the diversity in our community.

00:26:10.370 --> 00:26:13.760
Infrastructure as a
space tends to just not

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:16.330
be as diverse as other parts
of software, in my opinion.

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:18.280
So we do our best
to ensure that we

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:20.030
add tons of diversity
scholarships

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:22.220
for a lot of our
KubeCon related events.

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:26.675
And I mean, last year I think
we threw over $300,000 last year

00:26:26.675 --> 00:26:29.300
in diversity scholarships, which
is the most of any open source

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:29.780
foundation.

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:32.090
And to continue to grow that
to make sure our community is

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:34.220
inclusive and diverse, I
think, is super important

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:35.780
and something that
I'm personally

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:38.367
passionate about at the
Linux Foundation and CNCF.

00:26:38.367 --> 00:26:40.700
MARK MANDEL: So if I'm either
a company or an individual

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:42.140
and I want to get involved,
either with the Linux

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:44.420
Foundation or CNCF,
where should they go?

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:45.600
What should they do?

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:46.520
CHRIS ANISZCZYK:
I mean, generally

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:48.110
most people get involved
at the project level.

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:49.430
It's like, oh, I use GraphQL.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:50.888
I'm going to go
contribute to that.

00:26:50.888 --> 00:26:52.830
Or I use Spinnaker, I'm
going to go find that.

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:54.560
And then most of the times,
a lot of these projects

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:56.102
are just project
first, and they just

00:26:56.102 --> 00:26:58.670
happen to live in the
Foundation that kind of holds

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:01.950
the trademark or IP or whatever.

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:04.585
So just go to the projects that
you normally contribute to.

00:27:04.585 --> 00:27:05.960
You may be surprised
that they're

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:08.750
part of the Linux Foundation
and you have no specific idea.

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:09.140
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
There's probably

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:10.550
people who work on
this stuff and have

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:12.720
no idea what the Foundation
does or anything of that nature.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:13.090
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah.

00:27:13.090 --> 00:27:14.540
We do our best to
try to message,

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:17.090
but our whole
philosophy is to let

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:18.590
the projects do
what they're good at

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:19.882
and try to stay out of the way.

00:27:19.882 --> 00:27:22.140
We don't, like, force crazy
governance or anything.

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.500
We're just basically kind
of like referees and neutral

00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:27.660
holders of IP to ensure
people behave, essentially.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:29.400
It's kind of-- imagine
a sports match.

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:31.400
It wouldn't be fun if you
were playing in a game

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:35.253
and one team had an advantage
over another or something

00:27:35.253 --> 00:27:37.170
and the referee wasn't
really doing their job.

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:39.110
And so we try to make
sure the rules are fair

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:40.790
and these organizations
are supported.

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:42.350
MARK MIRCHANDANI: If I'm
a company, or even, like,

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:43.760
a group of people
who are like, oh,

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:44.840
I think we have a
great project that

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:46.910
would be a great fit somewhere,
is there like a [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:48.210
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: You can
go to linuxfoundation.org.

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:49.168
You go to projects.

00:27:49.168 --> 00:27:50.710
I think there's
like, Post a Project.

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:51.340
It's a link.

00:27:51.340 --> 00:27:52.370
Go check it out there.

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:55.010
You know, last year we hosted
events that probably nearly

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:57.260
had 35,000 or 40,000 people.

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:58.730
Go to Open Source
Summit, you know,

00:27:58.730 --> 00:28:01.370
we're doing one in
August in San Diego.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:01.977
Go to KubeCon.

00:28:01.977 --> 00:28:03.810
We host a lot of different
events out there.

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:06.290
So that's one way to get
involved with the community.

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:07.310
But go to the website.

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:08.210
We have a lot of information.

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:08.780
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:10.400
Before we finish up,
is there anything

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:11.570
we haven't covered,
something you

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:13.528
want to make sure that
the listeners know about

00:28:13.528 --> 00:28:14.577
before we get done?

00:28:14.577 --> 00:28:15.410
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: No.

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:17.240
I mean, a lot of
times, the simple thing

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:20.000
is people get confused at,
like-- the Linux Foundation,

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:21.500
obviously Linux is
a big part of it.

00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:22.792
But we do a lot more than that.

00:28:22.792 --> 00:28:24.417
You know, a lot of
people don't realize

00:28:24.417 --> 00:28:25.640
we support letsencrypt.org.

00:28:25.640 --> 00:28:28.883
We fix the Node, io, js things--

00:28:28.883 --> 00:28:30.050
now that's actually Open JS.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:33.860
So now the Node.js foundation
and the JSF slash jQuery folks

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:35.060
all merge into one happy--

00:28:35.060 --> 00:28:35.315
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh, nice.

00:28:35.315 --> 00:28:36.398
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: --entity.

00:28:36.398 --> 00:28:39.170
So just that we do a lot more
than I think people realize.

00:28:39.170 --> 00:28:40.970
And so check out our
website and kind of go

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:42.350
to our projects page,
and you'll be surprised

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:43.460
what's actually on there.

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:44.570
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Have
you considered the tagline

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:45.530
"More than just Linux"?

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:47.600
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: I
lost that war of,

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:50.280
like, maybe we should
rename the foundation.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:50.780
And then--

00:28:50.780 --> 00:28:52.130
MARK MANDEL: Or could we just
get some stickers, I think?

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:52.850
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Linux Foundation--

00:28:52.850 --> 00:28:53.510
more than just Linux.

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:54.520
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Yeah, exactly.

00:28:54.520 --> 00:28:55.020
Yeah.

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:56.802
No, every time you
get a free SSL cert,

00:28:56.802 --> 00:28:59.010
you know, send a little love
to the Linux Foundation.

00:28:59.010 --> 00:29:00.130
We helped make that happen.

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:01.760
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well,
thank you very much for that.

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:02.220
MARK MANDEL: Thank you so much.

00:29:02.220 --> 00:29:03.200
CHRIS ANISZCZYK: Thanks
for the conversation.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:03.810
This has been fun.

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:04.420
MARK MANDEL: Cheers.

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:05.900
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, so
that was a great conversation

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:06.410
with Chris.

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:08.420
Obviously, thanks so much,
Chris, for coming in.

00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:09.590
Super, super cool to hear.

00:29:09.590 --> 00:29:11.600
I didn't know much about
all these open source

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:15.120
foundations and kind of why they
were so important in the world.

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:17.150
And obviously, with all
these different people

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:18.748
kind of working
together, I can see

00:29:18.748 --> 00:29:20.540
why they might need a
little bit of support

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:21.560
from the higher layer.

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:22.070
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:23.210
Definitely love that.

00:29:23.210 --> 00:29:27.020
Well, let's get stuck into
the question of the week.

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:29.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:33.070
What I want to know is,
if I've got Cloud SQL,

00:29:33.070 --> 00:29:35.560
how do I connect that to
my serverless thingos?

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:35.960
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, I--

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:36.940
MARK MANDEL: That's the
technical term, by the way.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:37.310
Thingos.

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:37.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The thingos.

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:38.160
Yeah.

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:41.030
See, here at Google, we have
lots of thingos for you to use.

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:41.130
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:41.830
[CHIMING]

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:43.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, if
you're not using thingos,

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:45.430
but instead you're using
something like, oh,

00:29:45.430 --> 00:29:47.650
I don't know, the recently
announced Cloud Run--

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:49.775
pretty good offering, I
think, for most people--

00:29:49.775 --> 00:29:51.400
it turns out it's
becoming a lot easier

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:52.360
to connect in these things.

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:54.777
So traditionally, you might
have something like, you know,

00:29:54.777 --> 00:29:58.028
opening up your SQL to public
IP address, which is a fantastic

00:29:58.028 --> 00:29:59.320
idea and everyone should do it.

00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:00.110
[BUZZER]

00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:00.610
Do not.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:02.455
MARK MANDEL: Do not do that.

00:30:02.455 --> 00:30:05.110
MARK MIRCHANDANI: The
sarcasm did not come across.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:07.540
Yeah, clearly there's a lot
of security implications

00:30:07.540 --> 00:30:09.730
with opening up a database
to the outside world.

00:30:09.730 --> 00:30:10.720
[CHIMING]

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:13.010
So oftentimes, people
don't want to do that.

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:14.770
So in the case of Cloud
Run or in the case

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:16.187
of serverless
offerings, it can be

00:30:16.187 --> 00:30:17.800
kind of tricky to
understand, well,

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:19.510
you can't just whitelist a
few IP users or something

00:30:19.510 --> 00:30:20.010
like that.

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:21.670
It's not as easy to connect.

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:23.560
Well, thankfully,
there's a cool blog post

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:26.080
where Cloud Run talks about
some recent additions they've

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:29.170
made since the kind of the
next open beta for everybody

00:30:29.170 --> 00:30:30.040
to try out.

00:30:30.040 --> 00:30:32.800
And one of them is just a
very really quick command line

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:35.023
argument you push in called
Add Cloud SQL instances.

00:30:35.023 --> 00:30:35.940
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:37.690
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Couldn't be much easier.

00:30:37.690 --> 00:30:39.773
So if you're running Cloud
Run on the kind of just

00:30:39.773 --> 00:30:41.648
hosted version, which
has all the serverless,

00:30:41.648 --> 00:30:43.810
then you can kind of just
add in that command line.

00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:46.460
You pass in your Cloud SQL
instance and you're good to go.

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:48.305
If you're running Cloud
Run on Kubernetes,

00:30:48.305 --> 00:30:49.960
so you have a little
bit more structure

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:52.120
and that's kind of the
Knative aspect of it,

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:55.180
then it's actually pretty
easy to follow a different set

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:56.710
of instructions,
but you're adding

00:30:56.710 --> 00:30:58.250
a proxy in front of Cloud SQL.

00:30:58.250 --> 00:31:00.730
So you take that proxy, you
connect to that instead,

00:31:00.730 --> 00:31:03.250
and you still kind of maintain
your private cloud SQL.

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:04.970
So all the details
are in the blog post.

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:06.357
It's actually two
lines, I think,

00:31:06.357 --> 00:31:07.690
if you want add it to Cloud Run.

00:31:07.690 --> 00:31:08.905
So super, super easy.

00:31:08.905 --> 00:31:11.530
And I think that that will open
up a lot more access for people

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:14.120
to run databases along
with their serverless apps.

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:14.870
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:15.770
Very, very cool.

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:17.878
All right, well, before
we wrap up today, Mark,

00:31:17.878 --> 00:31:18.670
what are you up to?

00:31:18.670 --> 00:31:19.060
What are you doing?

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:20.110
What cool things are you doing?

00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:20.710
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, I'm working

00:31:20.710 --> 00:31:22.570
on a lot of video
projects, so hoping

00:31:22.570 --> 00:31:25.360
to come out and view some
customers on the east coast

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:25.990
soon.

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:27.595
And just in general,
you know, I try

00:31:27.595 --> 00:31:29.720
to live my life without
moving as much as possible.

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:31.180
MARK MANDEL: As
much as possible.

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:32.140
No arm waving.

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:32.950
No leg shaking.

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:34.367
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So far, so good.

